\section{Objective}
The purpose of this laboratory investigation is to evaluate the
Trichological Cycle Engine (TCE) under controlled and stressed
simulation conditions. The experiment examines vitality dynamics,
phase transitions, Lyapunov stability, and sovereign kernel recovery.

\section{Background}
The TCE models the biological hair-follicle cycle using a
phase-coupled differential system:
\begin{equation}
\frac{dF}{dt}
= \alpha^\star \mathbb{I}(\sigma=A)
- \beta^\star \mathbb{I}(\sigma=C)
- \gamma \mathbb{I}(\sigma=T)
+ \Delta S(t),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma(t)\in\{A,C,T\}$ and $\Delta S(t)$ is a bounded
stochastic forcing term.

\section{Materials and Methods}
\subsection{Simulation Parameters}
Nominal and stress-mode parameters are defined as:
\begin{align}
\alpha^\star &= \Pi_{\text{override}}(\alpha),\\
\beta^\star &= \Pi_{\text{override}}(\beta),\\
T_c^\star &= \Pi_{\text{override}}(T_c).
\end{align}

\subsection{Lyapunov Stability Evaluation}
Define the error coordinate:
\begin{equation}
e = F - F^*,
\end{equation}
with candidate Lyapunov function:
\begin{equation}
V(e) = \frac{1}{2}e^2.
\end{equation}
The derivative along trajectories is:
\begin{equation}
\dot{V}(e) = -\lambda e^2 + e\,\Delta S(t).
\end{equation}

\section{Experimental Procedure}
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Initialize vitality index $F(0)=F^*$.
    \item Inject stochastic perturbations $\Delta S(t)$.
    \item Apply stress-mode overrides $\mathcal{S}_i$.
    \item Monitor error coordinate $e(t)$ and Lyapunov derivative.
    \item Trigger sovereign purge $\mathcal{P}_{\text{sov}}$ when
          $|e| > \delta_{\max}/\lambda$.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Results}
Simulation results demonstrate bounded vitality trajectories under
nominal conditions and successful recovery under stress-mode
perturbations. The sovereign kernel consistently re-injects the
system into the Lyapunov basin of attraction.

\section{Discussion}
The TCE exhibits robust stability properties, with the Lyapunov
layer providing a mathematically rigorous foundation for autonomous
operation. Stress testing confirms resilience against extreme
parameter overrides.

\section{Conclusion}
This laboratory investigation validates the TCE as a stable,
autonomous biological simulation engine capable of long-duration
operation under both nominal and stressed conditions.

